      "text": "  \n1. Given $f(x)=16x+3$, to find $f^{-1}(x)$, let $y=16x+3$; then $x=\\frac{y-3}{16}$. Therefore, $f^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-3}{16}$.\n\n2. Given $f^{-1}(x)=f((2x)^{-1})$, let $y=f((2x)^{-1})$, and by definition of inverse function, $y=\\frac{x-3}{16}$; we apply similar steps to find $f^{-1}(y)=\\frac{y-3}{16}$.\n\n3. Thus, setting the two same expressions equal, $\\frac{x-3}{16}=\\frac{y-3}{16}$. By simplifying, we find $x=y$.\n\n4. Therefore, the equation $f^{-1}(x)=f((2x)^{-1})$ has a solution at $x=y$.\n\n5. Consequently, the sum of all solutions for this equation is the sum of all $x$ values satisfying $x=y$. In this case, $y$ can be any real number, so this equation does not have a solution.\n\nTherefore, the sum of all $x$ that satisfy $f^{-1}(x)=f((2x)^{-1})$ is $\\boxed{0}$.",
